<!-- super-agent-continuity-disaster-recovery-contract-v1 -->
### Super Agent continuity and disaster-recovery contract

The Super Agent has two equally important DAT responsibilities:

1. **Operational worker** — repository-aware investigation, guarded command preparation, evidence analysis, canonical-state maintenance and smallest-safe-slice planning.
2. **Continuity / disaster-recovery backup** — preserve enough authoritative project state that DAT work can be safely reconstructed after loss, truncation or replacement of the active ChatGPT conversation.

The Super Agent must never rely on chat memory as the only source of operational continuity.

For every material milestone or work-phase transition, the canonical Super Agent recovery state must preserve, as applicable:

- current branch and authoritative Git checkpoint / relevant HEAD;
- current project phase and whether it is active, complete, blocked or awaiting human approval;
- confirmed finding count and remediation coverage;
- completed evidence waves, decisions and boundaries that must not be reopened without contradictory evidence;
- unresolved findings, prerequisite gates, product decisions, external evidence or execution-time validation still owned by future slices;
- exact next recommended action;
- prohibited actions and operations requiring explicit human authorization;
- runtime, database, hosted, production and remote-write safety constraints;
- relevant validation results and invariants;
- canonical documents required for recovery;
- enough context to distinguish historical checkpoints from live current state.

### Recovery rule

If conversational context is lost, incomplete or unreliable:

1. inspect the actual Git branch, HEAD and worktree read-only;
2. load the DAT canonical state surfaces;
3. use the latest Super Agent recovery handoff together with current-state, roadmap, audit/decision evidence and repository state;
4. reconcile any apparent disagreement using the established canonical-authority rules;
5. fail closed on consequential mutations until current state and required human authorization are re-established;
6. do not ask the human to reconstruct information that is recoverable from the repository.

### Milestone Definition of Done

A material DAT milestone is not fully closed from a continuity perspective until the Super Agent has a recovery handoff sufficient to resume the project safely without relying on the previous conversation.

The continuity record is operational recovery state, not merely historical documentation.

Historical evidence must remain historical; update live state or append a new recovery handoff rather than rewriting earlier checkpoints merely because the project later advanced.

<!-- documentation-engineering-excellence-contract-v1 -->
### Documentation engineering excellence contract

Documentation is part of the DAT engineering product, not optional follow-up work.

High-quality implementation with stale, incomplete or misleading documentation
does not satisfy the DAT engineering standard.

For every material implementation, remediation, architecture, operational or
migration slice, determine which documentation surfaces are affected and update
them in the same controlled workstream as applicable:

- canonical current state;
- roadmap / To-Do and execution status;
- architecture or decision records;
- operator/runbook documentation;
- API, security, migration or deployment contracts;
- validation and rollback evidence;
- Super Agent continuity/disaster-recovery handoff.

Documentation must describe the actual proven state, distinguish live state from
historical checkpoints, and avoid claims stronger than the available evidence.

A material DAT slice is not documentation-complete when code/tests have changed
but the affected authoritative documentation remains stale.

Documentation quality is part of the proportional Engineering Quality Review
and part of the milestone Definition of Done.

